{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3419","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3419","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3419\",\"parent_revision\":\"69e9198bfd03e33928da2958dbc0c05b1125e4376405faddff6a9ffb2e7af240\",\"root_revision\":\"69e9198bfd03e33928da2958dbc0c05b1125e4376405faddff6a9ffb2e7af240\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-09-04\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`FileCache._work_item_snapshots()` (`file_cache.py`), `parsing.py:448`, and `_CacheStateStore._salvage_field` (`file_cache_state.py`) each independently implement the same \\\"glob a directory / iterate a collection, try/except per entry, log and skip\\\" salvage policy \u2014 with no shared helper, and the three copies already disagree on which exceptions they catch: `file_cache.py` catches `(ValueError, YAMLError, OSError)`, `parsing.py:448` catches `(YAMLError, OSError, ValueError, KeyError)`, and `_salvage_field` catches only `pydantic.ValidationError` (for a differently-shaped, list-based collection).\\n\\nPR #3405's own root bug \u2014 one uncaught exception type killing a whole batch \u2014 was already independently missing from two of these three call sites before that PR started (confirmed: `parsing.py` and `file_cache_state.py` were not touched by any of #3405's five commits). Nothing currently stops a future fix from being applied to only one of the three sites again, the same way #3405 itself only touched one.\\n\\nNot fixed as part of #3405 because designing the right shared abstraction (a `salvage_glob(dir, pattern, loader)`-style helper, or similar) that cleanly covers both a directory-glob shape and a list-collection shape, with a single correct exception set, is design work \u2014 not a mechanical extraction \u2014 and touches three files across two different subsystems, well beyond a crash-fix PR's scope.\\n\\nUser-provided context: worth checking during grooming whether all three call sites can actually share one signature, or whether the dict-vs-list shape difference means two helpers (one per shape) is the more honest design.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Code review on PR #3405 (cache-crash fix)\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-09-04\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"2516702140177207fefb0228c261ba833cb5dc050538848f2304d3ea2693fa00\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABStol3g\"}"}